To examine the impact of various punching techniques on HCF behavior, specimens were cut parallel to the RD. Four punch types were used: flat, rooftop (RT), humped bottom (HB), and a hybrid humped bottom bevel (HBB). Each punch was used to create a 15-mm-diameter hole using a displacement rate of 6.7 mm/s. A clearance of 8 pct was applied for the first three punch types, while the clearance was increased to 12 pct for the HBB punch. The HB and HBB punches had a punch height of 1 and 5 mm, respectively. The configurations of the punches and the resulting punched specimens are shown in Figure  1 (c). The stress concentration factor for these geometries was determined to be  K t  = 2.62 using the following equation [ 32 ] : 1 K t = 2 + 0.284 1 - d W - 0.6 1 - d W 2 + 1.32 1 - d W 3 , \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
